(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[13] = {"n":13,"set":"B","level":"Intermediate","band":"B1 55% · B2 45%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Artificial islands","p":["For most of history, the shape of a coastline was something people had to accept. Today, however, engineers can create entirely new land in the sea. Artificial islands, built by humans rather than formed by nature, are now used for homes, airports, ports, tourist resorts and even energy production. Some of the most ambitious projects in the world have been built in the Gulf region.","The most common method of building an artificial island is called land reclamation. First, engineers study the sea floor carefully to understand the depth of the water, the type of soil and the movement of waves and currents. Then, large ships called dredgers suck up sand from the sea bed in another area and spray it through pipes onto the chosen site, slowly building up a mound that rises above the surface. In many projects, the sand is surrounded by walls of large rocks, called breakwaters, which protect the new land from waves and erosion. Finally, the sand must be compacted, or pressed down firmly, so that it can safely support buildings.","One famous example is Palm Jumeirah in Dubai, which was built in the shape of a palm tree and can be seen from space. It required about ninety-four million cubic metres of sand and seven million tonnes of rock. Another example is Kansai International Airport in Japan, built on an island in Osaka Bay because there was not enough flat land near the city. In Saudi Arabia, several new coastal projects on the Red Sea include islands developed with careful attention to the marine environment.","Artificial islands offer several advantages. They can create space in crowded cities, provide land for airports away from homes, and attract tourism and investment. However, they also present serious challenges. Construction can be extremely expensive, and the new land may sink slowly over time as the sand settles. Kansai Airport, for example, has sunk by several metres since it opened, and engineers have had to raise parts of it.","The environmental impact is another concern. Dredging can make the water cloudy, blocking sunlight that coral and sea grass need to survive. Changes to the coastline can also alter currents, causing erosion in other places. Marine biologists have warned that some projects have damaged coral reefs and the habitats of fish and turtles.","As sea levels rise because of climate change, some countries are considering artificial islands not just as luxury developments but as a way to protect their populations. Engineers are now experimenting with new designs, including floating islands and structures that encourage marine life to grow on them. The challenge for the future will be to balance human needs with the health of the oceans."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how artificial islands are built and their benefits and problems","the history of Dubai","how coral reefs grow","the world's busiest airports"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage explains methods, examples, advantages and challenges.","h":"بناء الجزر الاصطناعية ومزاياها ومشكلاتها."},{"n":2,"s":"What is the first step in land reclamation?","o":["spraying sand onto the site","building breakwaters","compacting the sand","studying the sea floor"],"k":3,"ty":"sequence","e":"'First, engineers study the sea floor carefully'.","h":"دراسة قاع البحر."},{"n":3,"s":"What is the purpose of breakwaters?","o":["to provide sand for the island","to protect the new land from waves and erosion","to help ships reach the island","to create beaches for tourists"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"حماية الأرض من الأمواج."},{"n":4,"s":"The word 'compacted' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["washed away","made wet","pressed down firmly","made larger"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The passage explains it: 'or pressed down firmly'.","h":"مضغوط."},{"n":5,"s":"Why was Kansai Airport built on an island?","o":["The government wanted a tourist attraction.","The sea was too shallow elsewhere.","There was not enough flat land near the city.","Planes cannot land near mountains."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"قلة الأرض المستوية."},{"n":6,"s":"Why does the writer mention that Kansai Airport has sunk?","o":["to show that Japan has many earthquakes","as an example of a challenge facing artificial islands","to explain how planes land","to compare it with Palm Jumeirah"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"It illustrates land settling over time.","h":"مثال على التحديات."},{"n":7,"s":"According to paragraph 5, how can dredging harm coral?","o":["It makes the water too cold.","It removes all the fish.","It adds salt to the water.","It makes the water cloudy and blocks sunlight."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"تعكير الماء وحجب الضوء."},{"n":8,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an advantage of artificial islands?","o":["creating space in crowded cities","lower prices for food","land for airports away from homes","attracting tourism"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Food prices are not mentioned.","h":"أسعار الطعام غير مذكورة."},{"n":9,"s":"What can be inferred from the last paragraph?","o":["Artificial islands may become more important as sea levels rise.","Artificial islands will stop being built.","Floating islands already house millions of people.","Climate change has no effect on coasts."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Some countries consider them for protection.","h":"قد تزداد أهميتها."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's overall position?","o":["Artificial islands should be banned.","Artificial islands are useful but must be planned with care for the environment.","Artificial islands have no disadvantages.","Only rich countries should build them."],"k":1,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'balance human needs with the health of the oceans'.","h":"مفيدة مع العناية بالبيئة."}]},{"t":"The psychology of motivation","p":["Why do some people work hard to achieve their goals while others give up easily? What makes a student study late into the night or an athlete train every morning before sunrise? Psychologists have been studying motivation, the forces that start, direct and maintain our behaviour, for more than a century, and their findings can help us understand ourselves and others.","One important distinction is between extrinsic and intrinsic motivation. Extrinsic motivation comes from outside rewards or pressures: we work for money, grades, praise or to avoid punishment. Intrinsic motivation comes from within: we do something because we find it interesting, enjoyable or meaningful in itself. A child who reads because she loves stories is intrinsically motivated, while one who reads only to win a prize is extrinsically motivated.","Rewards can be useful, but research suggests they can sometimes reduce intrinsic motivation. In a well-known experiment, children who enjoyed drawing were divided into groups. Some were promised a certificate for drawing, while others were not. Later, when they were given free time, the children who had received rewards spent less time drawing than the others. It seemed that the reward had changed drawing from a pleasure into a job. Psychologists call this the 'overjustification effect'.","According to self-determination theory, developed by Edward Deci and Richard Ryan, people are most motivated when three basic psychological needs are met. The first is autonomy, the feeling that we have choice and control over our actions. The second is competence, the sense that we are capable and making progress. The third is relatedness, the feeling of being connected to and supported by others. When these needs are satisfied, people tend to show more effort, creativity and well-being.","Another influential idea is the 'growth mindset', described by psychologist Carol Dweck. People with a fixed mindset believe that intelligence and talent cannot change much, so they may avoid challenges for fear of failure. People with a growth mindset believe that abilities can be developed through effort and good strategies. As a result, they are more likely to persist after setbacks and see mistakes as opportunities to learn. However, later studies have shown that the effects of mindset training in schools are often smaller than early research suggested.","These theories have practical implications. Teachers and managers can increase motivation by offering meaningful choices, giving specific feedback on progress and creating supportive relationships. Individuals can motivate themselves by setting clear, achievable goals, breaking large tasks into smaller steps and reminding themselves why the work matters to them."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","o":["to describe the life of Carol Dweck","to explain some important ideas about motivation","to argue that rewards should be banned","to teach children how to draw"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage presents several theories of motivation.","h":"شرح أفكار عن الدافعية."},{"n":12,"s":"Which example shows intrinsic motivation?","o":["working to earn money","studying to avoid punishment","training to win a prize","reading because you love stories"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 gives this example.","h":"القراءة حبًا في القصص."},{"n":13,"s":"What happened to the children who received rewards for drawing?","o":["They later spent less time drawing in their free time.","They became better artists.","They refused to accept the certificates.","They drew more than the other children."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 describes the result.","h":"رسموا أقل لاحقًا."},{"n":14,"s":"The 'overjustification effect' refers to","o":["children drawing too much","teachers giving too much praise","students working without rewards","a reward reducing the pleasure of an activity"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The reward changed drawing from a pleasure into a job.","h":"المكافأة تقلل المتعة."},{"n":15,"s":"According to self-determination theory, which is NOT one of the three basic needs?","o":["autonomy","wealth","competence","relatedness"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Wealth is not listed.","h":"الثروة ليست منها."},{"n":16,"s":"What does 'autonomy' mean in paragraph 4?","o":["working alone without others","earning high grades","following strict rules","having choice and control over one's actions"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"The passage defines it.","h":"الاستقلالية."},{"n":17,"s":"How do people with a growth mindset tend to view mistakes?","o":["as proof that they lack talent","as opportunities to learn","as something to hide","as the fault of others"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"فرص للتعلم."},{"n":18,"s":"Why does the writer mention later studies in paragraph 5?","o":["to prove that growth mindset is false","to praise Carol Dweck's research","to show that the effects of mindset training may be limited","to describe experiments with animals"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"Later studies found smaller effects.","h":"لبيان محدودية الأثر."},{"n":19,"s":"Which strategy for self-motivation is mentioned in the last paragraph?","o":["working without any breaks","avoiding difficult goals","breaking large tasks into smaller steps","comparing yourself with others"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"The last paragraph lists this strategy.","h":"تقسيم المهام."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards the theories discussed?","o":["generally positive but aware of their limits","completely critical","uninterested","certain that they are all proven"],"k":0,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"The writer explains them and notes limits of mindset research.","h":"إيجابي مع الوعي بالحدود."}]},{"t":"Al-Jazari and his machines","p":["In the early thirteenth century, in the city of Diyarbakir in what is now south-eastern Turkey, an engineer named Badi' al-Zaman Abu al-'Izz ibn Isma'il ibn al-Razzaz al-Jazari completed a remarkable book. Its title can be translated as 'The Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices'. Today, al-Jazari is regarded as one of the most important engineers of the medieval world, and some historians describe him as a father of modern robotics.","Al-Jazari worked for more than twenty-five years in the service of the Artuqid rulers of the region. He designed water-raising machines, clocks, fountains, musical devices and automata, which are machines that move by themselves. His book describes about fifty devices in careful detail. Unlike many earlier writers, he included clear, coloured drawings and step-by-step instructions, so that other craftsmen could build the machines themselves.","Water was a major concern in the dry regions of the Islamic world, and several of al-Jazari's inventions were designed to lift water for irrigation and household use. One of his machines used a crankshaft, a device that converts rotating motion into back-and-forth motion. This system made it possible to pump water efficiently, and historians consider it one of the earliest known uses of this mechanism in a machine.","Al-Jazari's clocks were among his most impressive creations. His famous 'elephant clock' combined ideas and symbols from different cultures, including an Indian elephant, a Chinese dragon and an Egyptian water system, reflecting the wide connections of the Islamic world. Every half hour, a small figure moved, a ball dropped, and a sound announced the time. The clock was powered by a bowl that slowly sank in a tank of water, an ingenious method of measuring time.","He also designed entertaining automata, such as a boat with mechanical musicians that floated on a lake and played music for royal guests, and a hand-washing machine in which a figure offered water, soap and a towel. Although these devices were created to amuse and impress, they relied on sophisticated mechanical principles, such as programmable sequences, gears and valves, which later became central to engineering.","Al-Jazari's work shows that the Islamic world was a centre of technological innovation during the Middle Ages. His careful method of recording designs helped preserve knowledge, and modern engineers have successfully rebuilt several of his machines from his descriptions. His elephant clock has been reconstructed and can be seen today in museums, including one in Dubai."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"Why do some historians call al-Jazari 'a father of modern robotics'?","o":["He built the first computer.","He invented electricity.","He wrote the first book about robots in English.","He designed machines that could move by themselves."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"He designed automata, machines that move by themselves.","h":"صمم آلات ذاتية الحركة."},{"n":22,"s":"What made al-Jazari's book different from many earlier works?","o":["It was written in Latin.","It described only clocks.","It contained no technical details.","It included clear drawings and step-by-step instructions."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"رسومات وتعليمات واضحة."},{"n":23,"s":"The word 'automata' in paragraph 2 refers to","o":["water tanks","royal guests","machines that move by themselves","musical instruments"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The passage defines it.","h":"آلات ذاتية الحركة."},{"n":24,"s":"Why were water-raising machines important in the Islamic world?","o":["Rulers wanted to build lakes for boats.","Many regions were dry and needed water for irrigation.","Water was used to power clocks only.","Rivers flowed too fast."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"المناطق الجافة."},{"n":25,"s":"What does a crankshaft do?","o":["It measures the time.","It produces musical sounds.","It cleans water.","It converts rotating motion into back-and-forth motion."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 defines it.","h":"يحوّل الحركة الدورانية."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the design of the elephant clock reflect?","o":["the wide cultural connections of the Islamic world","the power of the Artuqid army","the importance of animals in farming","the rivalry between India and China"],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"It combined symbols from different cultures.","h":"الصلات الثقافية الواسعة."},{"n":27,"s":"How was the elephant clock powered?","o":["by a horse turning a wheel","by wind blowing through a tower","by a team of workers","by a bowl slowly sinking in a tank of water"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"وعاء يغرق ببطء."},{"n":28,"s":"What point does the writer make about al-Jazari's entertaining automata?","o":["Although designed to amuse, they used advanced engineering principles.","They were useless toys.","They were never built.","They were copied from Europe."],"k":0,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Paragraph 5 makes this point.","h":"مبادئ هندسية متقدمة."},{"n":29,"s":"What evidence shows that al-Jazari's descriptions were accurate?","o":["His machines are still used in factories.","Modern engineers have rebuilt his machines from them.","His book was translated into many languages.","Rulers paid him for twenty-five years."],"k":1,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Engineers successfully rebuilt several machines.","h":"إعادة بناء آلاته."},{"n":30,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It introduces al-Jazari, then describes his main types of machines and his legacy.","It compares al-Jazari with a European inventor.","It tells events in a single day.","It lists problems and solutions."],"k":0,"ty":"organization","e":"Intro → water machines → clocks → automata → legacy.","h":"تقديم ثم آلات ثم إرث."}]},{"t":"Bamboo as a building material","p":["When most people think of strong building materials, they think of steel and concrete. Yet across Asia, Latin America and Africa, millions of people live and work in buildings made from a material that grows naturally and quickly: bamboo. In recent years, architects and engineers around the world have become increasingly interested in bamboo as a sustainable alternative to traditional materials.","Bamboo is not a tree but a type of giant grass. Some species can grow up to a metre in a single day, making bamboo one of the fastest-growing plants on Earth. While a hardwood tree may take forty years or more to reach a size suitable for building, bamboo can be harvested after just three to five years. Because it grows back from its roots after cutting, a bamboo forest does not need to be replanted.","Despite its light weight, bamboo is remarkably strong. Its hollow, tube-like structure, divided by solid sections called nodes, gives it great strength in relation to its weight. In tension, some species are comparable to certain types of steel, and bamboo is also flexible, which allows buildings to bend rather than break during earthquakes and strong winds. For this reason, bamboo houses have survived earthquakes in countries such as Colombia and Ecuador that destroyed many concrete buildings.","Bamboo also has environmental benefits. Growing bamboo absorbs carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, and producing it requires much less energy than manufacturing steel or cement, which are responsible for a large share of global carbon emissions. Bamboo forests also help to prevent soil erosion on hillsides because of their extensive root systems.","However, bamboo has some disadvantages. Untreated bamboo can be attacked by insects and fungi and may rot if it remains wet, so it must be treated, often with natural salts, and protected from rain and direct contact with the ground. Bamboo poles also vary in size and shape, which makes standard engineering calculations more difficult. In many countries, building codes do not yet include rules for bamboo, so architects may struggle to obtain permission for large bamboo structures. In addition, some people associate bamboo with poverty and temporary housing.","To overcome these problems, researchers have developed engineered bamboo products, such as laminated boards made from strips of bamboo glued together. These products have consistent sizes and strength and can be used like timber. Famous examples, such as the Green School in Bali, show that bamboo can be used to create beautiful, modern buildings. If its challenges can be addressed, bamboo may play an important role in more sustainable cities of the future."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Bamboo is weaker than all other materials.","Bamboo grows only in Asia.","Bamboo has great potential as a sustainable building material.","Steel and concrete are no longer used."],"k":2,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage presents bamboo's strengths, weaknesses and future.","h":"إمكانات الخيزران."},{"n":32,"s":"According to paragraph 2, why is bamboo considered sustainable?","o":["It grows quickly and regrows from its roots.","It never needs water.","It can be imported cheaply.","It is a type of hardwood tree."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Harvested in 3–5 years and grows back from its roots.","h":"سريع النمو ويتجدد."},{"n":33,"s":"What gives bamboo its strength in relation to its weight?","o":["its very large leaves","the chemicals used to treat it","its hollow structure divided by solid nodes","its heavy wood"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"البنية المجوفة والعقد."},{"n":34,"s":"Why have bamboo houses survived earthquakes?","o":["Bamboo houses are always very small.","Earthquakes do not happen in bamboo forests.","Bamboo is flexible and bends rather than breaks.","Bamboo is heavier than concrete."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Flexibility allows buildings to bend.","h":"المرونة."},{"n":35,"s":"The word 'extensive' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["weak","widespread","short","dry"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Bamboo has widespread root systems.","h":"واسع الانتشار."},{"n":36,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a disadvantage of bamboo?","o":["It can be attacked by insects.","Poles vary in size and shape.","It is too expensive to grow.","Building codes often do not include it."],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Cost of growing is not mentioned.","h":"التكلفة غير مذكورة."},{"n":37,"s":"What problem do architects face in many countries?","o":["obtaining permission because building codes lack bamboo rules","finding enough bamboo to buy","training workers to cut bamboo","transporting bamboo by sea"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 mentions building codes.","h":"صعوبة الحصول على التراخيص."},{"n":38,"s":"What is the purpose of engineered bamboo products?","o":["to make bamboo cheaper to grow","to replace natural bamboo forests","to provide consistent sizes and strength","to protect bamboo from earthquakes"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"Laminated boards have consistent sizes and strength.","h":"توحيد الحجم والقوة."},{"n":39,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Green School in Bali?","o":["to show that bamboo can create modern, attractive buildings","to describe a school for farmers","to criticize bamboo buildings","to explain how bamboo is treated"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"It is an example of beautiful modern use.","h":"مثال على المباني الحديثة."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the writer's view of bamboo's future?","o":["very limited","certain to replace steel completely","of no interest to architects","promising if its challenges are solved"],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'If its challenges can be addressed, bamboo may play an important role'.","h":"واعد مع حل التحديات."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"How long ______ for the bus? You look frozen!","o":["are you waiting","have you been waiting","did you wait","do you wait"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have you been waiting — Duration up to now: present perfect continuous.","h":"المضارع التام المستمر."},{"n":42,"s":"The new stadium ______ when the World Cup starts.","o":["will have been completed","will complete","is completing","completes"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have been completed — Future perfect passive.","h":"المستقبل التام المبني للمجهول."},{"n":43,"s":"It was ______ an interesting lecture that nobody left early.","o":["so","too","very","such"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"such — such + a/an + adjective + noun.","h":"such an."},{"n":44,"s":"The scientist ______ discovery changed medicine won a Nobel Prize.","o":["who","whose","which","whom"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whose — whose for possession.","h":"whose."},{"n":45,"s":"If he ______ harder last term, he would be at university now.","o":["studied","had studied","would study","has studied"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had studied — Mixed conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":46,"s":"I'm not used to ______ on the left side of the road.","o":["drive","drove","driving","be driving"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"driving — be used to + -ing.","h":"be used to + -ing."},{"n":47,"s":"She had her house ______ last month.","o":["paint","painting","to paint","painted"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"painted — Causative: have something + past participle.","h":"التركيب السببي."},{"n":48,"s":"The ______ you leave, the less traffic you will meet.","o":["earliest","more early","early","earlier"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"earlier — the + comparative, the + comparative.","h":"كلما … كلما."},{"n":49,"s":"You ______ have seen Khalid at the mall. He was in Dammam all day.","o":["must","can't","might","should"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"can't — Impossible past deduction: can't have.","h":"استنتاج النفي."},{"n":50,"s":"I wish my neighbours ______ so much noise at night.","o":["wouldn't make","don't make","won't make","hadn't made"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wouldn't make — Annoyance: wish + would.","h":"wish + would للتذمر."},{"n":51,"s":"By the end of this year, my parents ______ married for thirty years.","o":["will be","are","will have been","have been"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have been — Future perfect with duration.","h":"المستقبل التام."},{"n":52,"s":"The teacher warned the students ______ late again.","o":["to not being","not being","not to be","don't be"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"not to be — warn + object + not to + verb.","h":"warn + not to."},{"n":53,"s":"______ having little money, they travelled around the world.","o":["Although","Despite","However","Because of"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Despite — Despite + -ing.","h":"Despite + -ing."},{"n":54,"s":"Very ______ people attended the meeting, so it was cancelled.","o":["little","a few","much","few"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"few — Countable, negative: few.","h":"few."},{"n":55,"s":"The committee ______ to announce its decision next week.","o":["expects by","is expecting by","expected by","is expected"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"is expected — Passive reporting structure: is expected to.","h":"is expected to."},{"n":56,"s":"Every morning, my grandmother ______ make bread for the whole family.","o":["was","used","would","uses"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"would — would for repeated past actions.","h":"would للعادة."},{"n":57,"s":"Abha, ______ is famous for its mild weather, attracts many tourists in summer.","o":["which","that","where","who"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"which — Non-defining clause for a place as subject: which.","h":"which."},{"n":58,"s":"Only after the meeting ______ the seriousness of the problem.","o":["they realized","did they realize","they did realize","realized they"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did they realize — Only after … at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Only after."},{"n":59,"s":"He regrets ______ to the doctor sooner.","o":["not going","not to go","to not go","didn't go"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"not going — regret + -ing for past actions.","h":"regret + -ing."},{"n":60,"s":"Should you need any help, ______ me.","o":["please contact","you contacted","you would contact","contacting"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"please contact — Inverted first conditional: Should you …, imperative.","h":"شرط مقلوب."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) quality of the (B) roads in our city (C) have improved (D) recently.","o":["quality","roads","have","recently"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"have — The quality is singular: has improved.","h":"الفاعل مفرد."},{"n":62,"s":"(A) Despite the traffic (B) was heavy, we (C) arrived (D) on time.","o":["Despite","was","arrived","on"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Despite — A clause needs Although.","h":"Although مع الجملة."},{"n":63,"s":"If I (A) were you, I (B) would (C) apologize to her (D) immediate.","o":["were","would","apologize","immediate"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"immediate — An adverb is needed: immediately.","h":"الظرف: immediately."},{"n":64,"s":"She (A) has been (B) work here (C) since she (D) graduated.","o":["has been","work","since","graduated"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"work — Present perfect continuous: has been working.","h":"has been working."},{"n":65,"s":"The museum (A) what I (B) visited last summer (C) was (D) fascinating.","o":["what","visited","was","fascinating"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"what — Relative pronoun for a thing: that / which.","h":"that / which."},{"n":66,"s":"He (A) can't (B) have (C) went home already; his car (D) is still outside.","o":["can't","have","went","is"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"went — can't have + past participle: gone.","h":"التصريف الثالث: gone."},{"n":67,"s":"I (A) look forward to (B) hear from you (C) at your (D) earliest convenience.","o":["look forward","hear","at","earliest"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"hear — look forward to + -ing: hearing.","h":"بعد to هنا -ing."},{"n":68,"s":"No sooner (A) had she (B) arrived (C) than the phone (D) has rung.","o":["had","arrived","than","has rung"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"has rung — No sooner had … than + past simple: rang.","h":"الماضي البسيط: rang."},{"n":69,"s":"(A) Each of the (B) students (C) have to submit a (D) report.","o":["Each","students","have","report"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"have — Each of … takes a singular verb: has.","h":"Each مفرد."},{"n":70,"s":"The new regulations (A) will introduce (B) next month (C) to reduce (D) traffic.","o":["will introduce","next","to","traffic"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"will introduce — Passive is needed: will be introduced.","h":"المبني للمجهول."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M13-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What does the traveller need to do?","o":["buy a new ticket","wait until tomorrow","go to the ticket counter","show the stamped ticket to the driver"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'just show the driver this stamp'.","h":"إظهار التذكرة المختومة."}],"t":"A missed bus connection","sc":["Situation: A traveller is speaking to an information officer at a bus station. (66 words)","Man: Excuse me. My bus from Taif arrived late, and I've missed my connection to Jeddah. What can I do?","Woman: Don't worry. The next bus to Jeddah leaves in forty minutes, at three fifteen. Your ticket is still valid, because the delay wasn't your fault.","Man: Do I need to change my ticket at the counter?","Woman: No, just show the driver this stamp. I'll mark your ticket now."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M13-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What does the teacher ask the student to send?","o":["her exam results only","her CV and information about the scholarship","the reference letter","a photo"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'Could you send me your CV and a short description of the scholarship?'","h":"السيرة الذاتية ووصف المنحة."}],"t":"Asking for a reference letter","sc":["Situation: A student is speaking to her former teacher. (63 words)","Woman: Ms. Amal, I'm applying for a scholarship, and I need a reference letter. Would you be willing to write one for me?","Woman: I'd be happy to. When do you need it?","Woman: The deadline is the twentieth, so in about two weeks.","Woman: That's fine. Could you send me your CV and a short description of the scholarship? It'll help me write a stronger letter."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M13-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"How can students follow today's lecture?","o":["by attending another class","by reading the textbook only","by asking the teaching assistant","by watching a recording online"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'He's recorded it instead, and we can watch it online.'","h":"تسجيل عبر الإنترنت."}],"t":"A cancelled lecture","sc":["Situation: Two university students meet outside a lecture hall. (59 words)","Man: Why is the hall empty? Isn't the economics lecture at nine?","Man: Didn't you see the email? Dr. Sami is ill, so today's lecture is cancelled. He's recorded it instead, and we can watch it online.","Man: Oh, I didn't check my email this morning. Is there still a quiz next week?","Man: Yes, the quiz hasn't changed. It's still on Tuesday."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M13-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"Why does the shop give the customer a different brand?","o":["The first brand is sold out.","The customer asked for a cheaper one.","They have had several complaints about the first one.","The new brand is on sale."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"'we've had a few complaints about this one'.","h":"شكاوى على النوع الأول."}],"t":"A faulty phone charger","sc":["Situation: A customer is returning an item to an electronics shop. (64 words)","Man: I bought this charger two days ago, but it only charges my phone very slowly, and it gets extremely hot.","Woman: That sounds like a fault. It could be dangerous, so please don't use it again.","Man: Can I exchange it?","Woman: Certainly. We'll give you a different brand, because we've had a few complaints about this one. It's slightly more expensive, but there's no extra charge."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M13-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What must the student do to borrow goggles?","o":["pay a small fee","sign her name on a list","ask the head teacher","bring a note from home"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'you have to sign for them'.","h":"توقّع على القائمة."}],"t":"Borrowing lab goggles","sc":["Situation: Two students are in a science laboratory. (58 words)","Woman: I've forgotten my safety goggles. The teacher won't let me do the experiment without them.","Woman: There's a box of spare goggles in the cupboard at the back, but you have to sign for them.","Woman: Where do I sign?","Woman: On the list on the cupboard door. Just write your name and return them at the end of the lesson."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M13-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"Where can the man find a parking space?","o":["on the underground levels","on the roof","outside the mall","near the entrance"],"k":1,"ty":"location / relationship","e":"'there are spaces on the roof'.","h":"على السطح."}],"t":"A crowded car park","sc":["Situation: A man is speaking to a car park attendant at a shopping mall. (56 words)","Man: Excuse me, I've been driving around for fifteen minutes and can't find a space.","Man: It's very busy today because of the sale. The underground levels are full, but there are spaces on the roof.","Man: How do I get to the roof?","Man: Follow the green signs and take the ramp on the right. It's open until midnight."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M13-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What changes about the reservation?","o":["the number of people and the time","only the number of people","only the time","the date"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Six people at eight thirty instead of four at eight.","h":"العدد والوقت."}],"t":"Confirming a dinner reservation","sc":["Situation: A restaurant employee calls a customer. (60 words)","Woman: Good afternoon. This is Al-Bustan restaurant calling to confirm your reservation for four people tonight at eight.","Man: Thank you for calling. Actually, there'll be six of us now. Is that possible?","Woman: Let me check. Yes, we can give you a larger table, but it will be at eight thirty instead of eight.","Man: That's fine. Eight thirty for six people, then."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M13-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"Why is the first student surprised?","o":["The essay was cancelled.","The deadline was extended.","The teacher lost his essay.","The deadline is earlier than he thought. Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"It is due tomorrow, not Thursday.","h":"الموعد أقرب مما ظن."}],"t":"A misunderstanding about a deadline","sc":["Situation: Two classmates are talking about an assignment. (58 words)","Man: I'm almost finished with the history essay. It's due on Thursday, right?","Man: No, it's due tomorrow. Dr. Hana changed the date last week because Thursday is a holiday.","Man: Tomorrow? I didn't know that! I still need to write the conclusion and check my references.","Man: You'd better start now, then. If you need help with the references, call me."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M13-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What is the main argument the students will make?","o":["Renewable energy is harmful.","Saudi Arabia should use more oil.","Solar power is too expensive to build.","Relying only on renewable energy is risky."],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"'our argument isn't that renewables are bad. It's that relying on them completely is risky.'","h":"الاعتماد الكامل محفوف بالمخاطر."},{"n":80,"s":"Why does the second student want to accept the other side's strongest points?","o":["She agrees with the other team completely.","Judges like teams that respond fairly.","It saves time in the debate.","The rules require it."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'The judges like teams that respond fairly.'","h":"الحكام يقدّرون الإنصاف."}],"t":"Planning a debate on renewable energy","sc":["Situation: Two members of the school debate club are preparing. (142 words)","Woman: The topic for next month's debate is 'Saudi Arabia should rely completely on renewable energy by 2050.' We're arguing against it.","Woman: That's interesting, because we probably agree with renewable energy in general.","Woman: Yes, but our argument isn't that renewables are bad. It's that relying on them completely is risky.","Woman: Good. We could argue that solar power depends on the weather and the time of day, so the country still needs other sources at night.","Woman: And that storing large amounts of electricity in batteries is still expensive.","Woman: We should also accept the other side's strongest points. The judges like teams that respond fairly.","Woman: Agreed. I'll research the cost of battery storage. Can you look at what other countries have done?","Woman: Sure. I'll focus on Germany and Australia, since they've invested heavily in renewables.","Woman: Let's meet on Sunday to put our arguments together."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M13-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"Why do the editors choose the solar car story for the cover?","o":["It is positive and likely to interest students.","The president refused an interview.","It is the longest article.","The car was built by the editors."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"It is positive and shows what students can achieve.","h":"إيجابية وتهم الطلاب."},{"n":82,"s":"What do they decide about printing?","o":["to publish online only","to print thousands of copies","to print a limited number and publish the full magazine online","to stop the magazine"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Print five hundred copies and put the full version online.","h":"طباعة محدودة ونسخة إلكترونية."}],"t":"Discussing a student magazine","sc":["Situation: Two editors of a university magazine are meeting. (147 words)","Man: The first issue of our magazine comes out next month. We still need to decide what goes on the front cover.","Man: What about the interview with the university president? It's the most important article.","Man: It's important, but I'm not sure it will attract students. Most of them want something more personal.","Man: Then how about the story of the engineering students who built a solar car? They won a national competition, and the photos are great.","Man: That's perfect. It's positive, and it shows what students can achieve.","Man: We should also decide whether to print the magazine or publish it online only.","Man: Printing is expensive, but students like having something they can hold. Let's print five hundred copies and put the full version online.","Man: Good compromise. I'll contact the printing company tomorrow for a price, and I'll ask the student union whether they can help us with the cost."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M13-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"What is the main disadvantage of Dr. Majid?","o":["He knows little about the topic.","He is very busy and often away.","He does not reply to emails at all.","He is not a famous expert."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'he's very busy and travels a lot'.","h":"مشغول ويسافر كثيرًا."},{"n":84,"s":"What does the student decide to do?","o":["choose Dr. Majid only","change her topic","choose Dr. Rana and consult Dr. Majid sometimes","ask for a third supervisor Talks and lectures"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'll choose Dr. Rana and ask Dr. Majid if I can consult him occasionally.'","h":"تختار د. رنا وتستشير د. ماجد."}],"t":"Choosing a dissertation supervisor","sc":["Situation: A postgraduate student is talking to a friend. (152 words)","Woman: I have to choose a supervisor for my dissertation by Friday, and I can't decide between Dr. Majid and Dr. Rana.","Woman: What's your topic?","Woman: Water use in agriculture. Dr. Majid is a famous expert on exactly that topic, but he's very busy and travels a lot.","Woman: And Dr. Rana?","Woman: Her research is in environmental policy, which is related but not exactly the same. But students say she replies to emails quickly and meets them every week.","Woman: Regular feedback is really important for a dissertation. I had a supervisor who was hard to reach, and it was very stressful.","Woman: That's what worries me. I think I'll choose Dr. Rana and ask Dr. Majid if I can consult him occasionally.","Woman: That sounds like the best of both. And remember to tell Dr. Rana about your plan from the beginning, so that she isn't surprised later.","Woman: Good point. I'll mention it in our first meeting."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M13-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"According to the lecturer, what actually travels from a guitar to our ears?","o":["the air around the guitar","the guitar string","a vibration passing through the air","tiny particles of sound"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'the air itself does not travel … only the vibration does'.","h":"الاهتزاز."},{"n":86,"s":"Why can't sound travel in space?","o":["There is no medium for it to travel through.","Space is too cold.","The distances are too great.","Light blocks the sound."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"Sound needs a medium and cannot travel in a vacuum.","h":"لا يوجد وسط."},{"n":87,"s":"Why does sound travel faster in steel than in air?","o":["Steel is warmer than air.","Steel vibrates at a higher frequency.","The molecules in steel are closer together.","Sound is attracted by metal."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"Molecules closer together pass the vibration more quickly.","h":"الجزيئات أقرب."}],"t":"Lecture: how sound travels","sc":["Situation: A physics lecturer is speaking to students. (230 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today we're going to look at how sound travels. Sound is a form of energy produced by vibrations. When a guitar string vibrates, for example, it pushes the air molecules next to it, which push the molecules next to them, and so on. In this way, a wave of pressure moves through the air until it reaches our ears. It's important to understand that the air itself does not travel from the guitar to us; only the vibration does. Because sound needs a medium, such as air, water or a solid, to travel through, it cannot travel in a vacuum. That's why space is completely silent, despite what you see in films. Sound also travels at different speeds in different materials. In air at room temperature, it moves at about three hundred and forty-three metres per second. In water, it travels more than four times faster, and in steel, about fifteen times faster. This is because the molecules are closer together, so the vibration passes more quickly from one to the next. Temperature matters too: sound travels slightly faster in warm air than in cold air. Finally, we hear sounds as high or low depending on their frequency, which is the number of vibrations per second. Humans can hear frequencies between about twenty and twenty thousand vibrations per second, while dogs and bats can hear much higher sounds."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M13-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"Why should a CV make a strong impression quickly?","o":["Recruiters spend less than a minute on the first reading.","Companies only accept one-page CVs.","Computers reject long CVs.","Interviews are very short."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'most recruiters spend less than a minute on the first reading'.","h":"وقت القراءة الأولى قصير."},{"n":89,"s":"Why does the speaker recommend using numbers?","o":["They make the CV look longer.","They are required by law.","They help computers read the CV.","They make achievements more convincing."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'Numbers make your achievements more convincing.'","h":"تجعل الإنجازات مقنعة."},{"n":90,"s":"Why should applicants adapt their CV for each job?","o":["Employers and software look for specific skills and keywords.","Each company uses a different language.","CVs must be handwritten.","Employers want different photos."],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"Software scans for keywords; show the skills the employer wants.","h":"مطابقة المهارات والكلمات المفتاحية."}],"t":"Talk: how to write a CV","sc":["Situation: A human resources manager is speaking at a university careers day. (224 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you for coming. As a human resources manager, I read hundreds of CVs every year, and I can tell you that most recruiters spend less than a minute on the first reading of each one. So your CV must make a strong impression quickly. Here are my main tips. First, keep it short and clear. For a recent graduate, one page, or two at most, is enough. Use clear headings, such as Education, Experience and Skills, and a simple, professional font. Second, put your most relevant information first. If you have just graduated, your education and any internships should come near the top. Third, describe achievements, not just duties. Instead of writing 'responsible for social media', write 'increased the club's followers by forty per cent in six months'. Numbers make your achievements more convincing. Fourth, adapt your CV for each job. Read the job advertisement carefully and make sure your CV shows the skills the employer is looking for. Many large companies now use computer software to scan CVs for keywords before a person reads them. Fifth, check your spelling and grammar very carefully. A single mistake can suggest that you are careless. Finally, be honest. Never include false information; employers often check qualifications and references, and dishonesty can cost you the job even after you have been hired."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Nevertheless","Consequently","Instead","Furthermore"],"x":["Electric cars produce no exhaust fumes. ______, they are much quieter than petrol cars, which reduces noise in cities."],"k":3,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"A second advantage is added.","h":"إضافة."},{"n":92,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 4 – 1","3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) As a result, many people now choose to shop online.","(2) Online shopping has become increasingly convenient.","(3) Customers can compare prices and read reviews in seconds.","(4) Products are often delivered to their door within a day."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"topic → compare → delivery → As a result.","h":"تسلسل منطقي."},{"n":93,"s":"The new policy, (A) which was (B) announced yesterday, (C) have been (D) criticized by many experts.","o":["which","announced","have","criticized"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"The subject The new policy is singular: has.","h":"الفاعل مفرد: has."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 1","Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4"],"x":["(1) Volunteering offers many benefits to young people. (2) It allows them to develop practical skills such as teamwork and communication. (3) It also helps them build confidence and make new friends. (4) Many universities offer scholarships to excellent students. (5) Finally, volunteering experience can strengthen a job application."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Scholarships are not about the benefits of volunteering.","h":"المنح خارج الموضوع."},{"n":95,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Similarly","In fact","Therefore","For example"],"x":["Many people believe that eating late at night causes weight gain. ______, research suggests that the total number of calories matters more than the time of eating."],"k":1,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"In fact introduces information that corrects a belief.","h":"تصحيح الاعتقاد."},{"n":96,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Regular exercise has many benefits for physical and mental health.","Diabetes is a common disease.","Many people sleep badly.","Gyms are expensive in some cities."],"x":["______ It reduces stress, improves sleep and increases energy levels. Regular physical activity also lowers the risk of heart disease and diabetes."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list health benefits.","h":"فوائد الرياضة."},{"n":97,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) However, the team did not give up.","(2) Our school robotics team entered a national competition last year.","(3) In the first round, their robot broke down.","(4) They repaired it overnight and went on to win second place."],"k":2,"ty":"best order","e":"event → problem → However → outcome.","h":"حدث ثم مشكلة ثم إصرار ثم نتيجة."},{"n":98,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Financial education should therefore become part of every school curriculum.","Some banks offer accounts for teenagers.","Debt can be very stressful.","Mathematics is a difficult subject."],"x":["Learning to manage money is an important life skill. Young people who learn to budget, save and avoid unnecessary debt are more likely to achieve their goals. Unfortunately, many schools still do not teach these skills. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A conclusion that follows from the problem described.","h":"توصية مبنية على الفقرة."},{"n":99,"s":"If the government (A) invests more in public transport, fewer people (B) will (C) drive to work, and traffic (D) would decrease.","o":["invests","will","drive","would"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"First conditional consistency: will decrease.","h":"الشرط الأول: will."},{"n":100,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Pottery is made from clay.","Many tourists buy souvenirs.","Fewer young people are learning skills such as weaving, pottery and wood carving.","Workshops are usually held at weekends. Teacher Section — Mock 13"],"x":["Many traditional crafts are disappearing in Saudi Arabia. ______ To keep these skills alive, several organizations now offer training workshops for young people."],"k":2,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"The gap explains why the crafts are disappearing.","h":"تشرح سبب الاندثار."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
